-- title:
--   Lifted formal group law and its extension cocycle
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\mathcal O$ is a commutative ring and $p$ a prime such that $p$ is a non-zero-divisor in $\mathcal O$, $\mathcal O$ carries an algebra structure over $\mathbb Z/p$ whose structure map has kernel exactly the ideal $(p)$, and $\mathcal O$ is complete and separated for the $(p)$-adic filtration. For brevity, call a type $g$ in the same universe as $\mathcal O$, equipped with a commutative ring structure and an $\mathcal O$-algebra structure, an *admissible test algebra* when $p$ is a non-zero-divisor in $g$ and $g$ is $(p)$-adically complete; all quantifications over $g$ below range over admissible test algebras.
--
--   The input data are: a natural number $r$ and a Honda system $H_1$ for the element $p \in \mathcal O$ on the module $\mathcal O^{r}$, that is, $\mathcal O$-linear maps $F, V$ on $\mathcal O^r$ with $F\circ V = V\circ F = p\cdot\mathrm{id}$ together with a submodule $L \le \mathcal O^r$ satisfying the four Honda axioms (`sh1_le`, `sh1_ge`, `sh2'`, `sh3`); a family $G$ of commutative rings indexed by $v \in \mathbb N$, each a cocommutative Hopf algebra, finite as a module over $\mathbb Z/p$; bialgebra maps $s_v : G(v+1) \to G(v)$ which are surjective (`hs`), with $\operatorname{finrank}_{\mathbb Z/p} G(v) = p^{v r}$ (`hrankG`) and $\ker (s_v) =$ the $p^{v}$-torsion ideal [`PDivisibleGroup.Hopf.torsionIdeal`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L157) of $G(v+1)$ (`hkerG`), and such that the Cartier dual [`CartierDual (ZMod p) (G v)`](def/HopfAlgebra_CartierDual.html#L12), i.e. the $\mathbb Z/p$-linear dual of $G(v)$, is a local ring (`hunipG`). Further, additive maps $\pi_v : \mathcal O^r \to \mathrm{DieudonneModule}(\mathbb Z/p, p, G(v))$ are given which are surjective (`hπ`), have $\pi_v x = 0$ if and only if $x \in p^{v}\,\mathcal O^r$ (`hπker`), intertwine $F$ and $V$ of $H_1$ with the Frobenius and Verschiebung of the Dieudonné module (`hπF`, `hπV`), and are compatible with the transition maps in the sense that `DieudonneModule.map` of $s_v$ carries $\pi_{v+1}x$ to $\pi_v x$ (`hπs`).
--
--   Finally, $\mathcal S$ is a system of split coordinates for these data, `SplitCoordinates p r H₁ G s π`: it records an integer $d = \mathcal S.d$, heights $h^c, h^e$, two towers $G^c_v$, $G^e_v$ of Hopf algebras with transitions $s^c_v, s^e_v$, maps $q^c_v : G(v) \to G^c_v$, $\pi^e_v : G(v) \to G^e_v$, $\sigma_v : G^e_v \to G(v)$ and $\Theta_v : G(v) \to G^c_v \otimes_{\mathbb Z/p} G^e_v$, a formal group law $\Phi_0$ in $d$ variables over $\mathbb Z/p$ with coordinates $\kappa_v : \mathbb{Z}/p[[X_1,\dots ,X_d]] \to G^c_v$, a tower $E_v = \mathcal S.\mathrm{Et}\,v$ of Hopf $\mathcal O$-algebras with transitions $s^t_v$ and comparison maps $\theta^e_v : \mathbb Z/p \otimes_{\mathcal O} E_v \to G^e_v$, elements $\hat c_{i,k,v} \in E_v$, submodules $M^c, M^{et} \le \mathcal O^r$, a basis $\alpha$ of $L$ indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,d$, and families of power series $a_{i,n} \in \mathcal O[[X_1,\dots ,X_d]]$ and $\bar a_{i,n}$ over $\mathbb Z/p$. Two hypotheses constrain it: `hℒ : 𝒮.Lawful` (a long conjunction, summarised here) demands $h^c + h^e = r$, that each $G^c_v$ be local and each $G^e_v$ reduced, cocommutative and finite with $\operatorname{finrank}$ equal to $p^{v h^c}$, $p^{v h^e}$, surjectivity of $s^c_v,s^e_v,q^c_v,\pi^e_v,\kappa_v$ with transition kernels the $p^v$-torsion ideals, $\ker \pi^e_v =$ nilradical, $\pi^e_v\circ\sigma_v = \mathrm{id}$, $\ker q^c_v$ the image under $\sigma_v$ of the augmentation ideal, bijectivity of $\Theta_v$ together with its description as $(q^c_v\otimes\pi^e_v)\circ\mathrm{comul}$, formal unramifiedness of $G^e_v$, commutativity of $\Phi_0$, $\ker \kappa_v$ generated by the $p^v$-th iterate series of $\Phi_0$, compatibility of all these maps with the transitions, and the remaining clauses tying $\alpha$, $a_{i,n}$, $\hat c_{i,k,v}$, $\theta^e_v$ and the submodules $M^c, M^{et}$ together; and `hNF : 𝒮.NormalForm`, which requires that the linear part of the family $(a_{i,0})_i$ reduce modulo $(p)$ to the identity matrix, and that the entry $(i,j)$ of the linear part of $(a_{i,1})_i$ lie in $(p)$ whenever $j \le i$.
--
--   Two representability hypotheses for Fontaine's functor complete the input. For an admissible test algebra $g$, a *Fontaine pair* is an element $z$ of the product of $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal O}(L, \mathrm{Localization.Away}\,(p:g))$ and $\mathrm{Hom}_{+}(\mathcal O^r, \mathrm{UnipotentWittCovector}\,p\,(\mathbb Z/p \otimes_{\mathcal O} g))$; membership in the subgroup `fontaineFunctor p H₁ (ZMod p) includeRight` means that $z_2 \circ F$ is the Frobenius of $z_2$, that $z_2\circ V$ is the Verschiebung of $z_2$, and that for every $l \in L$ there is a unipotent Witt covector $Z$ over $g$ whose image along $g \to \mathbb Z/p \otimes_{\mathcal O} g$ is $z_2(l)$ and with $z_1(l) \equiv \mathrm{wUp}\,Z$ modulo the submodule $\mathrm{pSub}\,p\,g$ of $\mathrm{Localization.Away}\,(p:g)$. Call $z$ bounded when $z_2$ annihilates $p^{v_0}\mathcal O^r$ for some $v_0$. Say that a triple $(f,e,y)$, with $f : G^c_v \to \mathbb Z/p\otimes_{\mathcal O} g$ and $e : G^e_v \to \mathbb Z/p\otimes_{\mathcal O}g$ algebra maps over $\mathbb Z/p$ and $y : E_v \to g$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra map, *presents* $z$ at level $v$ when $z_2$ equals `DieudonneModule.eval` of the composite of $\Theta_v$ with $\mathrm{lift}(f,e) : G^c_v\otimes G^e_v \to \mathbb Z/p \otimes_{\mathcal O} g$, precomposed with $\pi_v$, and when $e \circ \theta^e_v = \mathrm{id}_{\mathbb Z/p}\otimes y$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hREP` asserts: for every admissible test algebra $g$ and every bounded Fontaine pair $z$ in the group above, there is a unique $x : \mathrm{Fin}\,d \to g$ such that every $x_j$ lies in the radical of $(p)$ and such that for every level $v$ and every triple $(f,e,y)$ presenting $z$ at level $v$ one has $f(\kappa_v(X_i)) = 1 \otimes x_i$ for all $i$, and $$z_1(\alpha_i) = \mathrm{wSeries}_p\big(n \mapsto \mathrm{adicEval}_{(p)}(x)(a_{i,n})\big) + \mathrm{wSeries}_p\big(k \mapsto y(\hat c_{i,k,v})\big)$$ for all $i$, where $\mathrm{wSeries}_p(b)$ denotes the $p$-adic limit of the partial sums $\sum_{n<N} p^{-n} b_n^{p^n}$ in $\mathrm{Localization.Away}\,(p:g)$ and $\mathrm{adicEval}$ is $(p)$-adic evaluation of a multivariate power series. The hypothesis `hREP'` is the existence counterpart: for every admissible test algebra $g$, every level $v_1$, every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $y_1 : E_{v_1} \to g$ and every $x : \mathrm{Fin}\,d \to g$ with all entries in the radical of $(p)$ for which some $f : G^c_{v_1} \to \mathbb Z/p\otimes_{\mathcal O}g$ satisfies $f(\kappa_{v_1}(X_i)) = 1\otimes x_i$, there exists a bounded Fontaine pair $z$ in the group which is presented at level $v_1$ by a triple $(f,e,y_1)$ with $f(\kappa_{v_1}(X_i)) = 1\otimes x_i$, and which satisfies the same characterisation as in `hREP` at every level: for all $v$ and all $(f,e,y)$ presenting $z$ at level $v$, $f(\kappa_v(X_i)) = 1\otimes x_i$ and $z_1(\alpha_i)$ is given by the displayed two-term $\mathrm{wSeries}$ formula.
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of a formal group law $\Phi$ in $d$ variables over $\mathcal O$, a proof that $\Phi$ is commutative, and a family $c_2(v) : \mathrm{Fin}\,d \to E_v \otimes_{\mathcal O} E_v$ for every $v$, subject to the following seven conditions. Write $c_2(f,f')_j := \mathrm{lift}(f,f')(c_2(v)_j) \in g$ for $\mathcal O$-algebra maps $f,f' : E_v \to g$, write $f * f' := \mathrm{lift}(f,f')\circ \mathrm{comul}$ for the convolution product on $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal O\text{-alg}}(E_v,g)$, write $\varepsilon$ for the composite of the counit of $E_v$ with $\mathcal O \to g$, and write $u \boxplus_\Phi u'$ for the tuple $i \mapsto \mathrm{adicEval}_{(p)}(u \sqcup u')(\Phi_i)$ formed from two $d$-tuples in $g$.
--
--   (1) The reduction of $\Phi$ along $\mathcal O \to \mathbb Z/p$ is $\Phi_0$.
--
--   (2) For all $v$ and $i$, the map $s^t_v \otimes s^t_v$ carries $c_2(v+1)_i$ to $c_2(v)_i$.
--
--   (3) For all $v$ and $i$, $c_2(v)_i$ lies in the ideal generated by $p$ in $E_v \otimes_{\mathcal O} E_v$.
--
--   (4) Normalisation: for every admissible test algebra $g$, every $v$, every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $f : E_v \to g$ and every $j$, both $c_2(f,\varepsilon)_j = 0$ and $c_2(\varepsilon,f)_j = 0$.
--
--   (5) Symmetry: for every admissible test algebra $g$, every $v$, all $f,f' : E_v \to g$ and every $j$, $c_2(f,f')_j = c_2(f',f)_j$.
--
--   (6) Cocycle identity: for every admissible test algebra $g$, every $v$ and all $f,f',f'' : E_v \to g$, the $d$-tuples $c_2(f,f') \boxplus_\Phi c_2(f*f',f'')$ and $c_2(f',f'') \boxplus_\Phi c_2(f,f'*f'')$ coincide.
--
--   (7) Additivity of the logarithm up to the cocycle: for every admissible test algebra $g$, every $v$, all $f,f' : E_v \to g$, all $d$-tuples $x, x'$ in $g$ with entries in the radical of $(p)$, and every $i$, $$\xi_i\big(f*f',\; x \boxplus_\Phi (x' \boxplus_\Phi c_2(f,f'))\big) = \xi_i(f,x) + \xi_i(f',x'),$$ where $\xi_i(h,u) := \mathrm{wSeries}_p(n \mapsto \mathrm{adicEval}_{(p)}(u)(a_{i,n})) + \mathrm{wSeries}_p(k \mapsto h(\hat c_{i,k,v}))$ for an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $h : E_v \to g$.
--
--   Only existence of the pair $(\Phi, c_2)$ with these properties is asserted; no uniqueness is claimed here.
--
--   This is the construction step of Fontaine's lifting theory for Honda systems in the mixed (non-connected) case: out of a lawful system of split coordinates in normal form, and the two representability properties of Fontaine's functor on $(p)$-adically complete $\mathcal O$-algebras, it produces the commutative formal group law $\Phi$ over $\mathcal O$ lifting the connected law $\Phi_0$, together with the symmetric normalised $2$-cocycle $c_2$ which measures the extension by the étale part and corrects the additivity of the logarithm. It is used in the construction of the lifted $p$-divisible tower with a bijective comparison map to the Fontaine–Hodge data, [`Deformation.HondaSystem.exists_pDivisibleTower_bijective_map_mem_fontaineHodge_of_pDivisibleTower_zmod`](thm.html#Deformation.HondaSystem.exists_pDivisibleTower_bijective_map_mem_fontaineHodge_of_pDivisibleTower_zmod).
